      "title": "Polynomial compatibility on the Boolean cube from disjoint-support separators",
      "description": "<p>A complete affirmative deduction for Austrin's exact two-polynomial question, OWR 9/2025 printed p. 445 (30006210 / OWR-14299088-012). For every positive n,d and nonzero real multilinear f,g of degree at most d on the uniform sign cube, all full-norm relative coordinate influences at most 1/(4096d^8) guarantee a common nonzero vertex. Disjoint supports force maximum relative influence at least1/(1024d^8). The denominator includes the constant Fourier coefficient; arbitrary common zeros are allowed. Constants/exponent are not claimed optimal. General distribution formulations, other groups/measures and approximate supports are outside the asserted theorem.</p><p>This four-page note is an explicit target-specific application of the established Li-Li-Li-Liu 2026 disjoint-support separator and O'Donnell-Saks-Schramm-Servedio 2005 section3.3 Eq(6) real covariance inequality, with the classical Markov/Bernoulli presentation credited to Kothari and coauthors. Neither the separator nor covariance inequality is claimed new. The complete square-density conversion and extracted depth 16d^4 are supplied.</p><p>Extensive bounded primary priority audits found no earlier recorded complete target-specific deduction in the bodies inspected. They do not certify novelty, first discovery/publication or global current openness. The full ePrint 2022/218 and full 2023/570 followup were inaccessible; the official CRYPTO 2022 conference version was read and separately pinned. Other historical coverage and procedural-independence limits are documented. Dates and negative searches do not certify firstness or causal independence.</p><p>The source archive preserves all ten original PR artifacts, three distinct mathematical audits, three priority approaches, root checks, and a portable offline standard-library verification runner. Four exact integer/rational programs run in ordinary and optimized modes with all mathematical fields compared exactly and forty actual incorrect-result failures. Finite checks supplement the universal proof; they are not arbitrary-dimensional enumeration or formal proof certificates. Third-party source bodies are excluded.</p><p>AI tools were used extensively in proof development, writing, computation, reproduction, literature research and adversarial verification. This note and package are unrefereed. Internal AI-assisted reviews do not constitute external human peer review, journal acceptance or formal proof-assistant certification.</p>",
